


Jonah rubbed his uninjured arm. “There.”




Maren stopped beside him. “Cold air moving down the slope.”




“There’s no wind.”




She held a strip of flagging tape from her equipment pouch at waist height. The loose end moved once toward the river and then hung still. She raised it above her head. The higher air remained warmer.




“Cold pocket near the ground,” she said for the camera. “Short duration.”




Jonah aimed his light upslope.




The trees thinned near the crest. A pale vertical shape stood between two trunks, taller than the brush around it and narrow at the top. Jonah stopped so abruptly that Maren almost walked into him. His left hand came back against her forearm.




“Don’t go closer.”




She moved beside him, not past him. “What do you see?”




“Someone.”




The shape stood several dozen feet upslope. Its lower half disappeared behind brush. Jonah’s beam reached it through a gap in the leaves, and the upper edge suggested a head above one shoulder. Nothing moved.




Maren raised the camera and widened the frame to include the trail marker, Jonah, and the shape. Autofocus caught the nearer leaves. She switched to manual focus, but the pale form remained featureless.




“Can you see a face?” she asked.




“No.”




“Hands?”




“No.”




Maren kept the lens raised. “We’re a permitted film crew on the river trail. Are you lost?”




No one answered.




Crickets continued on the slope. A small animal moved through dry leaves below the trail and stopped when Jonah turned his light toward the sound. The pale shape remained above them.




Jonah called louder. “Can you hear us?”




Nothing changed.




Maren looked back at the curve they had just climbed. “Keep your light on it. I’m changing the angle. Do not leave the trail.”




She backed down eight steps with the camera still recording. The shape widened between the trunks. One edge flashed white when Jonah’s beam crossed it, then went dull again.




“Reflective surface,” she said.




She moved three more steps. A thin metal post appeared beneath the pale section.




Jonah lowered his shoulders but kept the light steady.




They advanced together along the marked trail until the curve opened a clear view of the crest. The shape resolved into the reflective back of a narrow trail sign turned away from them. Its post disappeared into the brush, and the sign faced a path on the opposite side of the rise.




Maren filmed the sign from the trail, then turned the camera through the surrounding trees and brush. Jonah swept his light across the slope. Neither of them saw a person move away. No second shape appeared.




They waited another thirty seconds.




Jonah let out a hard breath. “I thought it had shoulders.”




“So did I.”




Maren recorded the viewing angle, the reflective backing, and the leaves that had hidden the post. When they started downhill, Jonah stayed beside her instead of moving ahead.




The odor reached them near the bottom of the slope.




It was weak at first, damp soil beneath the cooler river air. Two steps later it thickened into a sweet, rotten smell that caught at the back of Maren’s throat.




“Stop,” Jonah said.




They stood still and tested the air. The smell was stronger on the river side of the trail. Maren aimed her light through the brush. Water had collected in a shallow depression below the bank. Pale fungal growth covered part of a fallen log. Several feet beyond it, something dark lay beneath nettles. A cluster of flies lifted from the shape and settled again.




Maren kept both feet on the trail and raised the camera.




“Animal?” Jonah asked.




“Likely. I can’t confirm it from here.”




He spoke into the handheld recorder. “Decay odor below the trail. Probable animal remains or stagnant water. We are not approaching.”




Maren marked the location on the official map with a grease pencil. They moved on together. The odor diminished within thirty feet, returned faintly when the trail bent toward the water, and disappeared on the next rise.




The river loop crossed another outcrop and reached a bench overlooking a broad bend. The county road lay somewhere beyond the opposite trees. A farm security light showed through a gap to the northwest. Its reflection trembled in the water and vanished whenever branches moved across the line of sight.




Maren stopped the camera beside the bench and checked the time. They had taken fourteen minutes from the shelter instead of ten. The next check-in was due at ten.




“We turn back at the junction,” she said.




Jonah looked toward the farm light. “The sound could carry from there.”




“It could,” Maren said. “Or from somewhere closer along the road.”




He kept the recorder aimed across the river.




Maren started the camera again and filmed the river bend, roadward trees, and light. “We have no source for the recorded call. The route includes river acoustics, road traffic, wildlife, and occupied property within hearing distance.”




Jonah moved out of frame.




She finished the statement and followed him.




They returned to the junction by the same route. The cold pocket did not recur on the rise. The reflective sign remained visible from the clearer angle. The carrion odor returned at the depression, weaker than before. A frog called immediately after Jonah stepped over a root. He looked toward the river but said nothing.




At the junction, Maren took the official map from its sleeve. The paper had softened at the folds from handling. Yellow highlighter marked their permitted route: shelter, river overlook, northern junction, and return loop. The preserve boundary formed an irregular shape around the trail system. Protected areas appeared as hatched blocks without public paths. The crime-associated area Halvorsen had identified sat outside the preserve, across the county road and farther west.




Jonah leaned against the trail post and checked the route sheet. “We’re north of the shelter now.”




“Yes.”




“The article’s grave is north.”




“The article puts it west in the coordinates.”




“It describes the rise north of the river.”




Maren removed the folded article printout from the map case. The page contained a simplified map beneath the cropped shelter photograph. A heavy red circle enclosed an old-cabin label, a grave symbol, a settlement grid, a quarry edge, and the words SITE OF 1974 ATTACK. A blue line labeled river curved through the bottom of the circle. A black path joined every feature.




She placed the official map over the printout. The plastic sleeve allowed the article’s dark labels to show through.




At first the two pages appeared close enough to support Jonah’s reading. Both contained a river bend, a north arrow, and a trail junction. Then Maren aligned the shelter symbol with the actual shelter.




The river on the article shifted beyond the official boundary.




She aligned the river bend instead. The shelter moved more than a mile north, into a hatched area with no public trail.




She aligned the county road. The settlement grid landed over the modern entrance prairie, while the quarry edge crossed the river.




“Hold the light here,” she said.




Jonah pointed his headlamp down. Maren rotated the printout beneath the official map. The article’s scale bar claimed that the shelter, quarry, settlement, crime area, and grave all fit within less than half a mile. On the preserve map, the same references—where they existed at all—occupied separate sections divided by the river, roads, and protected land. One fell outside the boundary.
